"OCC024 | making_significance | Tang edicts assigned robe colours to official ranks, with purple for the highest and green or blue for lower ranks, making dyed cloth a visible mark of rank. | An edict of 630 assigned robe colours to official ranks, purple for the third rank and above, crimson for the fourth and fifth, green for the sixth and seventh and blue for the eighth and ninth, making dyed cloth a visible mark of rank. | https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/定衣服制度奏（長興三年正月太常禮院）\nOCC024 | date_note | approx.; revised later in the Tang | edict of the 8th month, Zhenguan 4 (630); revised later in the Tang | https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/定衣服制度奏（長興三年正月太常禮院）\nOCC024 | confidence | medium | high | https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/定衣服制度奏（長興三年正月太常禮院）\nOCC024 | notes | Year and exact colour ladder to verify; rules changed several times. | Colour ladder quoted from the Zhenguan 4 edict in a Later Tang memorial (932); rules changed several times. | https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/定衣服制度奏（長興三年正月太常禮院）\n",
"OCC037 | making_significance | Mali's ruler travelled to Mecca through Cairo with large amounts of gold and, according to later sources, brought back the poet and builder al-Sahili, credited with work in Timbuktu and Gao. | Mali's ruler travelled to Mecca through Cairo with large amounts of gold and brought back the Andalusi poet al-Sahili, whom Ibn Khaldun credits with an audience hall in Mali and later tradition with mosques at Timbuktu and Gao. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Sahili\nOCC037 | notes | links: POL036; CIV008; NET010. Al-Sahili's role is debated. | links: POL036; CIV008; NET010. Architectural attributions to al-Sahili beyond the audience hall are weakly supported. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Sahili\nOCC057 | making_significance | The Spanish-led conquest, with Indigenous allies, destroyed Tenochtitlan and melted most Mexica gold and featherwork-adorned treasure into bars, so few pieces survive. | The Spanish-led conquest, with Indigenous allies, destroyed Tenochtitlan, and most Mexica goldwork was melted into bars, so few pieces survive. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Tenochtitlan\n",
